## Introduction

In Nigerian site language, "screeding" and "plastering" blur into one activity, but they are distinct operations that often happen on the same wall — one to build and level, the other to refine and finish. Ordering one when you need the other, or skipping the one that matters, is how walls end up either rough under smooth paint or expensively over-finished.

This guide draws the practical line between the two: purpose, mix designs, thickness, application sequence, when each is genuinely required, and what it means for cost when a contractor quotes "screed and plaster" as one item — or quietly drops one of them.

## The Line in One Sentence Each

**Plastering** builds and protects: it is the *structural* cement-sand skin of a masonry building — covering blockwork inside and out, straightening the wall's plane, protecting it from weather and wear, typically 12–15 mm and applied to raw blockwork.

**Screeding** refines and prepares: it is the *finishing* coat that follows plaster — a thinner cement-sand (or gypsum-based) pass that brings the wall to paint-ready flatness and surface quality, typically 3–10 mm, applied to a plastered (or otherwise fair) surface.

The sequence on a Nigerian wall is usually: blockwork → plaster (the build skin) → screed (the refine skin) → putty/primer/paint. On floors, "screed" additionally names the leveling bed under tiles — the word has two jobs, and context (wall vs floor) sorts them; this guide is about walls. The two trades on the same wall are why the terminology blurs: the same mason often does both, a week apart, and clients remember one word for both days.

## The Differences That Actually Matter

| | Plastering | Screeding |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Build, protect, straighten | Refine, tighten, paint-prepare |
| Substrate | Raw blockwork | Plastered / fair wall |
| Typical thickness | 12–15 mm (with render build-ups) | 3–10 mm |
| Mix design | Leaner (1:5–1:6 typical), workability for volume | Richer at the surface (1:3–1:4), hardness for finish |
| Surface ambition | Level and plumb, staffs and rules | Flat and tight, torch-and-palm standards |
| Failure signature | Hollows, deviation, weather damage | Dusting, crazing, paint telegraphing |
| Curing need | Yes — and exterior exposure demands it | Yes — and sun-stripping ruins it fastest |
| What follows it | Screed (indoors), weathering (outdoors) | Putty, primer, paint |

## When the Wall Genuinely Needs Both

The plaster-then-screed sequence earns its cost in the standard case: blockwork is neither flat nor fine enough for finishes, and the two coats fix the two different problems. Plaster corrects the wall's *geometry* — plane, plumb, and protection — while screed corrects its *surface* — texture, tightness, paint-readiness. Skipping plaster and "just screeding" onto raw blockwork fails twice: the screed cannot build 15 mm of geometry without cracking, and the blockwork is left without its weather skin outdoors. Skipping screed and painting straight off plaster saves one coat and shows under every evening light — the orange-peel of troweled plaster telegraphing through satin paint is Nigeria's most common "why does my paint look rough" answer.

**The practical rule:** rough blockwork or exterior exposure → both coats, in order. A genuinely fair, plastered interior wall may need only a screed touch before putty; an exterior wall almost never needs a screed (its "finish" is the plaster itself or a textured coating — see [interior vs exterior finishing](/learn/interior-vs-exterior-wall-finishing-differences)).

## When One Alone Is Honest

**Plaster alone is the finished wall outdoors.** External faces stop at a well-floated plaster or a textured coating over it; a smooth indoor-style screed outdoors is a maintenance liability, not a finish.

**Screed alone is honest on fair surfaces.** On a smooth concrete face, a sound existing plaster, or pre-cast panels, the wall may skip straight to a screed coat (with proper bonding — see [the bonding agents guide](/learn/best-bonding-agents-wall-screeding)). What is *not* honest is screed-as-plaster on raw blockwork — the thin coat pretending to do the thick coat's job, a decision whose invoice arrives as mapped cracks within the first season. (The related decision — when a wall needs plaster at all versus the finer gypsum options — is drawn separately in [skimming vs plastering](/learn/skimming-vs-plastering-understanding-options).)

## Reading the Quote — What "Screed and Plaster" Is Pricing

On the cost side, the two operations are separate labour and material lines, and quotes blur them in both directions:

- **The blurred single line** — "wall finishing, per m²" — can hide the missing screed. The check: ask what coats the per-m² rate includes, then hold the finished wall to a torch test. A quote priced without screed will pass a noon inspection and fail an evening one; the per-m² rate is not the problem, the missing coat is.
- **The dropped coat** appears in low bids as "plaster and paint" with screed quietly omitted; the paint then does the screed's job badly at a tenth of its lifespan. The check is the same: coats in writing, coat counts in the scope (see [the screeding costs guide](/learn/how-to-estimate-screeding-costs-nigeria) for pricing each line honestly).
- **The doubled coat** is the rarer abuse: a fair plastered wall quoted for a full levelling screed it does not need. The straightedge decides: gaps under 3 mm need a finishing pass, not a geometry campaign.

The quantities each line consumes differ too: plaster's bulk is sand-heavy and lean (1:5–1:6); screed's surface is cement-richer (1:3–1:4) — so the two lines price differently per m², and a quote that prices them identically has not thought about either — a warning this guide has now made twice, because it is the sentence that saves the most money in the whole blurred-terminology conversation. Run both lines through [the quantities guide](/learn/how-to-calculate-screeding-material-quantities) arithmetic and the market check does the rest.

## The Same Wall, Priced Both Ways — a Worked Sense-Check

For a 100 m² wall set (a moderate flat's internal faces), with plaster at its lean bulk mix and screed at its finishing mix, the honest material shape is: plaster consumes the sand volume (roughly a full trip+ and a few bags per 10 m² at 12–15 mm), screed consumes the cement surface (a small sand volume but a meaningfully richer bag count per 10 m² at 6–8 mm). Priced as one blurred line, the quote either under-carries the screed's richer bag count (and delivers a lean, dusty screed) or over-carries the plaster's bulk (and pads the bill). The two-line quote — plaster per m² at its own rate, screed per m² at its own, each priced from its own material shape — is the one that survives the arithmetic and the torch test together.

## The Sequence and the Payments

Because screed keys to plaster, the sequence is contractual, not stylistic: plaster, its cure, its tap-and-edge acceptance, *then* screed onto a sound, clean, cured base — then the paint schedule keyed to the screed's own moisture test. Payment gates follow the same line: plaster paid when its geometry passes, screed paid when its surface passes, paint paid when the five-check walk-through does (the full protocol: [the complete screeding guide](/learn/complete-guide-wall-screeding-professional) and [finishing quality standards](/learn/finishing-quality-standards-what-to-look-for)). A combined "finishing" payment with no gates pays for coats that were never verified — the oldest trick in the blurred-terminology book.


## The Terminology Map — What Your Site Actually Means

Because the blur costs money, here is how the words are used across Nigerian sites, so a quote can be decoded instead of trusted:

- **"Render"** is the exterior plaster skin (and sometimes the interior base pass) — the weather coat, lean mix, thick.
- **"Plaster"** indoors is the same family — the build skin on blockwork, straightened to staffs.
- **"Screed"** on walls is the refine coat over plaster (or the one-coat finish on a fair wall). **"Screed"** on floors is a different material doing the same word's work — the sand-cement leveling bed under tiles, 25–50 mm, with mesh where spans demand. Context (wall or floor) is the decoder.
- **"Skim"** is the gypsum family's version of the refine coat — the finish-paste pass over screed or plaster, and the third coat in the smoothest systems (see [skimming vs plastering](/learn/skimming-vs-plastering-understanding-options) for where it belongs).
- **"POP"** in finisher's language is ceilings, not walls — plaster of Paris cornices and boards; a "POP screed" quote is mixing categories (see the [POP ceiling guides](/learn/complete-guide-pop-ceiling-installation)).

The decoding habit for any quote: ask the tradesman to name the coats in order with thicknesses, and watch the words stabilize. "Screed and plaster" as one undifferentiated line is a warning; "plaster 12 mm, screed 6 mm, putty, primer, two coats" is a specification — and it is the sentence a professional produces without being asked twice, from the same memory that keeps the two coats' prices apart.

## The Floor Footnote — Where the Word Splits

The floor-screed deserves its one honest paragraph, because the misunderstanding is common on mixed-trade sites and in blurred quotes: the floor screed is *not* the wall screed at floor scale. It is a leveling bed (25–50 mm, lean mix, often mesh-reinforced over suspended slabs) whose job is geometry for the tile or finish above it — it is the *plaster logic* (build and level) applied underfoot. Its quantities, mixes, and curing are the tiling system's business ([the flooring guides](/learn/complete-guide-floor-tile-installation) cover it); its appearance in a wall-finishing conversation is the terminology tax that this map exists to refund.

## Closing

Plaster builds and protects; screed refines and prepares — different thicknesses, different mixes, different failure modes, different prices per m². Order both where blockwork needs both; order one honestly where the wall needs one; and make the quote name its coats before the wall wears them. The line between the two is the line between a wall that lasts and a wall that was described loosely — draw it in the scope, and every stage gate, payment, and paint can that follows will respect it. The client who can say "plaster at twelve, screed at six, putty, primer, two topcoats" is not quoting decoration — they are describing the wall the way its builder should, and the tradesman who hears that sentence quotes a wall, not a word.
